    <DataNode elementId="AT5G48880-MONOMER" textLabel="3-ketoacyl-CoA thiolase 5, peroxisomal" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q570C8" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="275" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Although the KAT5 protein is predicted to function as a 3-ketoacyl-CoA thiolase based on its sequence, the specific reactions catalyzed by KAT5 and the role that it plays &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; remain largely uncharacterized. A related protein, |FRAME: AT2G33150-MONOMER KAT2|, has been shown to function in wound-induced beta-oxidation and jasmonic acid biosynthesis |CITS: [15141068]|. KAT5 can functionally complement a &lt;i&gt;kat2&lt;/i&gt; mutant if it is expressed under the control of a constitutive promoter suggesting that this enzyme has similar biochemical capabilities |CITS: [11696182]|. And, the transcript levels of KAT5 rise in response to jasmonic acid and to wounding suggesting that this enzyme may play some role in wounding-induced JA biosynthesis |CITS: [15141068]|. On the other hand, based on transcript co-expression analyses, there is a competing prediction that KAT5 is involved in flavonoid biosynthesis |CITS: [17120136]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G48880-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT5G48880.2" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q570C8" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pid" value="AAM97120" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="Acetyl-CoA acyltransferase 5" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="Beta-ketothiolase 5" />
      <Property key="Synonym_3" value="Peroxisomal 3-oxoacyl-CoA thiolase 5" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-26847" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT5G48880.2" />
      <Property key="FunctionalAssignmentStatus" value=":AMBIGUOUS" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT5G48880" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_15141068" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_11696182" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_17120136" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT5G47720-MONOMER" textLabel="acetoacetyl-CoA thiolase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT5G47720-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="275" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">AACT1 has acetoacetyl-CoA thiolase activity based on its ability to complement a yeast &amp;Delta;&lt;i&gt;erg10&lt;/i&gt; mutant and &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays using recombinant protein |CITS: [22332816]|. Compared to its Arabidopsis ortholog |FRAME: AT5G48230 AACT2|, it has a 6-fold lower catalytic efficiency &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; (k&lt;sub&gt;cat&lt;/sub&gt; of 87 +/- 5 vs. 503 +/- 26 min &lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;) |CITS: [22332816]|. In Arabidopsis, acetoacetyl-CoA thiolases plays a major role in providing the necessary |FRAME: ACETOACETYL-COA acetoacetyl-CoA| for the cytosolic |FRAME: PWY-5910 mevalonate pathway| for isoprenoid production. However, given its higher catalytic efficiency and higher level of protein expression throughout much of the plant, |FRAME: AT5G48230 AACT2| appears to be the dominant enzyme &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt;. Consistent with this hypothesis RNAi plants with reduced levels of AACT2 have root phytosterol profiles that differ both qualitatively and quantitatively from wild type plants and accompanying phenotypic abornmalities such as decreased cell number, decreased cell size, increased sterility, and reduced seed yield |CITS: [22332816]|. In addition, &lt;i&gt;aact2&lt;/i&gt; mutants have defects in embryogenesis and male gamete formation. Meanwhile, &lt;i&gt;aact1&lt;/i&gt; mutant plants appear normal and have no obvious aberrant phenotypes. AACT1 promoter GUS experiments suggest that AACT1 expression is likely highest in the vascular system of various organs |CITS: [22332816]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G47720-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="acetyl-CoA acetyltransferase activity" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33541, RXN-6870" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT5G47720.2" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT5G47720" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_22332816" />
    </DataNode>
